<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Let's Pack for a Trip. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> A Trip to the Beach</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Pack for a Trip.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write instructions for simple tasks. interactive: Edit classmates' written work.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write instructions for tasks related to a beach trip (e.g., 'Pack a towel and sunscreen.'). Can develop short drafts about beach activities (e.g., 'At the beach, I swim.'). Can revise peers' instructions for clarity (e.g., add 'bring water' to the checklist).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 34%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; text-align: left; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of various beach items (e.g., sunscreen, towel, swimsuit, beach ball, bucket, flip-flops). Students call out the names. The teacher then asks, "What do you pack for a beach trip?" and writes student responses on the board. The teacher leads a quick chant: "Pack, pack, pack for the beach! What do you pack?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a swimsuit and says, "Please, pack your swimsuit." The teacher writes the sentence on the board, pointing to the word 'pack'. The teacher models the pronunciation of /p/ in 'pack' and /b/ in 'beach', emphasizing the sounds. The teacher then shows a simple written instruction like "Bring a towel." and asks students to spell 'towel' using their alphabet knowledge, guiding them to blend the sounds /t/ /aʊ/ /əl/. This reinforces phonemic awareness related to spelling.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'Pack your sunscreen,' do I put it in my bag or leave it at home?" (Expected answer: "Put it in your bag.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'Please bring your flip-flops' an instruction or a question?" (Expected answer: "An instruction.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple written checklist titled "My Beach Trip Packing List" on a large chart paper or projected on the board. The list contains 5-6 short, imperative sentences. Example: "1. Please pack your swimsuit. 2. Bring a towel. 3. Don't forget sunscreen. 4. Take a sun hat." It also includes a short paragraph like "I am going to the beach this weekend. I like to swim and build sandcastles. It is always sunny and fun."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The students will interpret and understand simple written instructions and short descriptions related to a trip to the beach.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the list aloud, pointing to each item. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is this list for?" (Expected: "A beach trip.") "What are you going to do at the beach?" (Expected: "Swim and build sandcastles.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The list says 'Forget your swimsuit.' True or False?" (False) "At the beach, I am going to relax. True or False?" (True, from the paragraph).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify and circle all the 'pack' and 'bring' verbs on the list.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing simple imperative instructions, using 'Present simple' and 'Going to' to describe activities. Focus on polite expressions with imperatives and target vocabulary: beach, towel, sunscreen, swimsuit, sun hat, flip-flops, pack, bring, forget, swim, relax, build.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Cloze & Word Building):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences where students fill in the blanks using target vocabulary or imperative verbs. For example: "Please _____ your _____. (pack/sunscreen)". Or, "At the beach, I _____ to swim." (like). The teacher also gives scrambled letters for 1-2 syllable words (e.g., 'a-n-d-s' for 'sand', 'a-p-c-k' for 'pack') and students write the correct word, practicing spelling and blending phonemes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Picture Prompts & Sentence Writing):</b> The teacher shows flashcards with beach items. For each flashcard, students write one instruction using an imperative. E.g., for a 'towel' picture, students write: "Please bring a towel." For a 'sun' picture, "Don't forget your sunscreen." The teacher circulates, checking for correct spelling (e.g., 'beach' /b/ and 'pack' /p/) and sentence structure.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Peer Checking for Spelling & Clarity):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student writes three simple instructions for packing for the beach on a small whiteboard or paper. Then they exchange their lists. Partner A checks Partner B's list for clear instructions and correct spelling, focusing on vocabulary words like 'sandy' and 'fun' and verbs like 'run' or 'collect'. Partner B does the same for Partner A. They identify any words that need correction (e.g., 'swimmsuit' instead of 'swimsuit').</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Guided Writing and Peer Editing</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will individually write a short "My Beach Trip Plan" document. First, they write a paragraph about what they are going to do at the beach this weekend, including 2-3 activities (e.g., "I am going to the beach. I am going to swim and collect shells."). Then, they will write a short checklist (3-4 items) of polite instructions for packing essential items, using imperatives (e.g., "Please bring your flip-flops."). They must include at least 5 vocabulary words from the list (towel, sunscreen, swimsuit, etc.) and use both imperative and 'going to' structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After drafting, students will exchange their "Beach Trip Plans" with a new partner. Each partner will act as an "editor," reading their peer's work and using a simple checklist to ensure clarity and completeness. They will suggest one item to add to the packing list if something is missing (e.g., "Maybe add 'Bring water'?") and circle any misspelled words they identify, focusing on the target words. This directly addresses the 'edit classmates' written work' objective and 'revise peers' instructions for clarity' learning outcome.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their revised "My Beach Trip Plan" (paragraph + checklist) to the teacher. The teacher collects the peer-edited work.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written instructions (Are they easy to understand?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of at least two imperative forms (e.g., 'Pack,' 'Bring').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of 'going to' for future plans.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Inclusion of at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., towel, sunscreen, swimsuit, beach).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility and basic spelling of common words (e.g., 'beach,' 'sand,' 'pack').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of peer revision (e.g., partner's suggestion/correction).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection before leaving.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write instructions for a beach trip: Yes / A little / No"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My friend helped me make my writing better: Yes / A little / No"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one new word I used in my writing today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy or difficult about writing instructions?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Today, the students successfully engaged in writing simple instructions and short paragraphs about their beach plans. The peer editing component worked well to highlight areas for improvement in clarity and spelling, particularly for words like 'sunscreen' and 'flip-flops'. Some students still struggled with consistently using 'going to' versus present simple for future plans, requiring further reinforcement. I will need to provide more sentence frames for the 'going to' structure in the next lesson to ensure all students feel confident using it.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing practice will prepare students for Lesson 5, where they will mediate and integrate all skills by creating a collaborative "Beach Trip Guide" for younger students, summarizing information and explaining activities, requiring clear written output.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Date: ________</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Time: 5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>